namespace gismo {
/**

\page C_interface C interface

The C interface exposes \gismo to `C` (and through it to
\ref Julia_interface "Julia", \ref MATLAB_interface "MATLAB" and
Fortran). It is part of the main library: every module keeps its C
functions in <tt>src/&lt;module&gt;/cinterface/</tt> (for example
<tt>gsNurbs/cinterface/gsCNurbsCreator.h</tt>), together with an umbrella
header <tt>Cgismo&lt;Module&gt;.h</tt>, and the type-blind core lives in
<tt>src/gsCInterface/</tt> (opaque handle types in <tt>gsCTypes.h</tt>,
the error firewall in <tt>gsCError.h</tt>).

Configure with <tt>GISMO_BUILD_CINTERFACE=ON</tt>: the C functions are
then compiled into the modules and the generated header
<tt>&lt;gsCInterface/Cgismo.h&gt;</tt> includes the umbrellas of all
modules (and of optional modules that ship a <tt>src/cinterface</tt>
directory). Plain-C programs include that header only; the examples in
<tt>src/gsCInterface/examples</tt> are built and run as tests with the
label <tt>gsCInterface</tt>.

Feature detection: <tt>gsC_hasModule("gsNurbs")</tt> tells whether a
module was compiled into the library or loaded at runtime,
<tt>gsC_modules()</tt> lists the compiled-in modules and
<tt>gsC_loadModules()</tt> loads the runtime modules of the module
directory (see \ref gsModules).

C++ exceptions never cross the C boundary: every entry point is wrapped
in <tt>GISMO_CAPI_BEGIN</tt> / <tt>GISMO_CAPI_END</tt>. On an error the
function returns a sentinel (NULL, -1, NaN or nothing) and the message
is available from <tt>gsCLastError()</tt> on the calling thread.



*/
}

